 for system boot time. Reduce this bottleneck by running our processor at 48 MHz through boot, until vboot hashing of RW completes.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:49583 TEST=Boot chell, verify vboot hash completes within 1 sec of EC boot and 'cbmem' delta between 'vboot select&amp;load kernel' and 'finished EC verification' is reduced to ~250 ms (which includes sysjump time). BRANCH=glados  Change-Id: I18d87e685b89decef761e51517bfcfc43dcf8ef0 Signed-off-by: Shawn Nematbakhsh &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/326792 Commit-Ready: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="326792" owner_email="shawnn@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/92/326792/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I5ba5237104bcea64da0246a0ffd00a59f3e1d141" commit="c2c2bbcfd9a5df11e0b8bdb196d9c9226778fd00" commit_message="CHROMIUM: Enable VMTests on pre-cq for kernel CLs  Since VMTests now pass on Strago we should have them run by the pre-cq.  We can also add in Glados as another pre-cq test as it also consumes this kernel.  BUG=None TEST=None  Change-Id: I5ba5237104bcea64da0246a0ffd00a59f3e1d141 Signed-off-by: Bernie Thompson &lt;bhthompson@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325030 Reviewed-by: Olof Johansson &lt;olofj@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Eric Caruso &lt;ejcaruso@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="325030" owner_email="bhthompson@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/30/325030/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I61a310c976488c47a51ff99855a0aea6bffc7b92" commit="1fef599c13e27f306807f00490ea55c48fc177fb" commit_message="[autotest] Handle invalid restricted group name.  On the servers where the restricted groups is set to something like &quot;USE SHADOW CONFIG&quot;, is_restricted_user will fail with a key error because &quot;USE SHADOW CONFIG&quot; is not a valid group.  This CL handle the error properly.  TEST=None BUG=None  Change-Id: I61a310c976488c47a51ff99855a0aea6bffc7b92 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/327062 Commit-Ready: Fang Deng &lt;fdeng@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Fang Deng &lt;fdeng@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Paul Hobbs &lt;phobbs@google.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Don Garrett &lt;dgarrett@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="327062" owner_email="fdeng@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/62/327062/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I2b027eef816949527138f7b8b53d5408fc823093" commit="45cddcb5cbfcad604bf229d8d0f0644f7247baa1" commit_message="Driver: isl9237: configures ACOK assertion to switching time  Isl9237 default setting sets 1.3s debounce time from ACOK assertion to switching. It's too long for EC being notified AC is present. Change the value to 150ms.  BUG=none BRANCH=glados TEST=In Kunimitsu system, plug in Zinger and capture timestamp when EC receive AC_PRESENT interrupt, it's reduced by ~1.2s.  Change-Id: I2b027eef816949527138f7b8b53d5408fc823093 Signed-off-by: li feng &lt;li1.feng@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/326844 Commit-Ready: Li1 Feng &lt;li1.feng@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Li1 Feng &lt;li1.feng@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="326844" owner_email="li1.feng@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/44/326844/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I13b7adbd9a3c14a1ddc6a1946a48a331ed7c3991" commit="5cdf6a7c79493134e14376d928cc11ba36696710" commit_message="UPSTREAM: arm64: mm: dump: add missing includes  The arm64 dump code is currently relying on some definitions which are pulled in via transitive dependencies. It seems we have implicit dependencies on the following definitions:  * MODULES_VADDR         (asm/memory.h) * MODULES_END           (asm/memory.h) * PAGE_OFFSET           (asm/memory.h) * PTE_*                 (asm/pgtable-hwdef.h) * ENOMEM                (linux/errno.h) * device_initcall       (linux/init.h)  This patch ensures we explicitly include the relevant headers for the above items, fixing the observed build issue and hopefully preventing future issues as headers are refactored.  BUG=chromium:583462 TEST=None (build)  Signed-off-by: Mark Rutland &lt;mark.rutland@arm.com&gt; Reported-by: Mark Brown &lt;broonie@kernel.org&gt; Acked-by: Steve Capper &lt;steve.capper@linaro.org&gt; Cc: Laura Abbott &lt;lauraa@codeaurora.org&gt; Cc: Will Deacon &lt;will.deacon@arm.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Catalin Marinas &lt;catalin.marinas@arm.com&gt; (cherry picked from commit 764011ca8247808e066a182bcfe42a2b14c99a9a) Signed-off-by: Guenter Roeck &lt;linux@roeck-us.net&gt; Signed-off-by: Olof Johansson &lt;olofj@chromium.org&gt;  Change-Id: I13b7adbd9a3c14a1ddc6a1946a48a331ed7c3991 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/326826 Commit-Ready: Olof Johansson &lt;olofj@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Olof Johansson &lt;olofj@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Andrew Bresticker &lt;abrestic@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="326826" owner_email="olofj@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/26/326826/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I6471a05a1f5f75b5c805e80190a3cc7ac96d7c3b" commit="98ac5c941f4ea5cee79cb06c0ddcf147e75700cc" commit_message="Kunimitsu: S0ix/S3 EC power improvement  USB2_OTG_ID and USB2_OTG_VBUSSENSE pins were floated due to open drain configuration. Improve EC power and remove power difference between S3 and S0ix. Improve EC power for SOC G3.  BRANCH=firmware-glados-7820.B BUG=none TEST=measure EC powers at S3/SOC-G3 and S0ix and check if there are difference.  Change-Id: I6471a05a1f5f75b5c805e80190a3cc7ac96d7c3b Signed-off-by: Kyoung Kim &lt;kyoung.il.kim@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/326901 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit 31bff74cff46bf80518cc269da304a1090cb7eea) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/326991 Commit-Ready: Kyoung Il Kim &lt;kyoung.il.kim@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Kyoung Il Kim &lt;kyoung.il.kim@intel.com&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="326991" owner_email="kyoung.il.kim@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/91/326991/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I4726854b7784e2e4a39b8cb74c350206d71f90df" commit="664fd2c5069590cd5f8860703b4a61bcf92ce5fe" commit_message="MEC1322 port80 Acknowledge interrupt  In normal operation the 16 bit timer number 1 is set up to count every microsecond, and every 1000 counts (i.e. every millisecond) to assert an IRQ (Interrupt Request). After a microsecond the IRQ is deasserted.when the count is again not at its limit.  The IRQ handler ignores the IRQ from the timer itself.  If the clock is stopped or the autoreload of the counter is disabled then the value of the count is left unchanged. If this count is the limit then the IRQ will remain asserted. For stopping the clock this is approximatly a 1 in 1000 chance, or is certain if the autoreload is disabled.  If the IRQ from the timer continues to be asserted, then the NVIC will continue to generate a fresh call to the IRQ handler as each previous exception completes.  The fix is to do what almost every IRQ handler does for almost every processor, and clear the request in the peripheral that is causing the interrupt, rather than hoping that the timer will clear it itself. This agrees with how the event timer is used. There may be a lurking bug in the system timer handler as well as it also expects the timer to clear its own IRQ.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:48499 TEST=Pass 2000s of continuous calls to port_80_interrupt_disable() / port_80_interrupt_enable() without WDT being triggered. Stop the autoreload and see it doesn't watchdog. BRANCH=glados  Change-Id: I4726854b7784e2e4a39b8cb74c350206d71f90df Signed-off-by: Icarus Sparry &lt;icarus.w.sparry@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/326781 Commit-Ready: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="326781" owner_email="icarus.w.sparry@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/81/326781/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><lkgm version="7910.0.0-rc3"/></manifest>